    "sourceMarkdown": "# K1 Unit 07: Graded Reading\n\n## Course Overview\n\n### Course Type\n\nGraded Reading\n\n### Course Purpose\n\nThis course supports leveled picture-book reading through picture walk, teacher read-aloud, echo reading, pointing, choosing, movement, pretend play, and short oral responses. Each lesson centers on one Level AA book.\n\n### Relationship to PowerUp\n\nPowerUp provides the broad unit theme: **空闲活动 / 运动、出行、采摘**. The graded reading books do not need to match PowerUp lesson-by-lesson; they provide leveled book language, picture-supported vocabulary, and light theme connections.\n\n### Unit Book Set\n\n| Week | Lesson Focus | Primary Book | Target Language |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| W1 | free-time activities and places | AA Summer Picnics | We go _____. / I see _____. |\n| W2 | free-time activities and places | AA The Baseball Game | We go _____. / I see _____. |\n| W3 | free-time activities and places | AA The Street | We go _____. / I see _____. |\n| W4 | free-time activities and places | AA The Trip | We go _____. / I see _____. |\n\n# Lesson 1: Level AA - Summer Picnics\n\n## Core Book: Level AA - Summer Picnics\n\n### Source\n\n- **Book:** Level AA - *Summer Picnics*\n- **PDF:** TBD\n- **Audio:** TBD\n- **Video:** TBD\n- **Lesson Plan:** TBD\n- **Worksheet:** TBD\n\n### Language Pattern\n\n#### Sentence Frame\n\n`Summer ____.`\n\n#### Key Vocabulary\n\nsummer hats, summer shoes, summer cups, summer plates, summer foods, summer sun, summer picnics, summer fun\n\n### Story Context\n\nChildren use *Summer Picnics* to explore free-time activities and places through pictures, repeated language, and a short classroom task.\n\n### Suggested Activities / Games\n\n1. Pack a Picnic Basket: children choose picnic cards such as hats, shoes, cups, plates, and foods, pack them into a basket, and name each item before the class pretends to go outside.\n2. Picnic Blanket Sort: children sort picnic items onto a blanket by wear, eat, or use, then choose one item to show and say.\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n- Look at the picture.\n- Point to the _____.\n- Say it with me.\n- Read with me.\n- What do you see?\n- Good reading.\n\n\n# Lesson 2: Level AA - The Baseball Game\n\n## Core Book: Level AA - The Baseball Game\n\n### Source\n\n- **Book:** Level AA - *The Baseball Game*\n- **PDF:** TBD\n- **Audio:** TBD\n- **Video:** TBD\n- **Lesson Plan:** TBD\n- **Worksheet:** TBD\n\n### Language Pattern\n\n#### Sentence Frame\n\n`this _____.`\n\n#### Key Vocabulary\n\nbase, baseball, bat, helmet, team, uniform\n\n### Story Context\n\nChildren use *The Baseball Game* to explore free-time activities and places through pictures, repeated language, and a short classroom task.\n\n### Suggested Activities / Games\n\n1. Floor Map Journey: place destination or vehicle cards around a floor map; children move a toy person/vehicle to one card and say or echo `this _____.`.\n2. Ticket and Station Game: children receive a ticket card, find the matching station, name the place or vehicle, and travel as a group to the next book location.\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n- Look at the picture.\n- Point to the _____.\n- Say it with me.\n- Read with me.\n- What do you see?\n- Good reading.\n\n\n# Lesson 3: Level AA - The Street\n\n## Core Book: Level AA - The Street\n\n### Source\n\n- **Book:** Level AA - *The Street*\n- **PDF:** TBD\n- **Audio:** TBD\n- **Video:** TBD\n- **Lesson Plan:** TBD\n- **Worksheet:** TBD\n\n### Language Pattern\n\n#### Sentence Frame\n\n`The ____.`\n\n#### Key Vocabulary\n\ngate, mailbox, sidewalk, fence (recurs from U4 L4 The Backyard), flowers (recurs from U4 L4 The Backyard), bike, friend (singular — a friend met on the street; recurs from U2 L3, U6 L4), street\n\n### Story Context\n\nChildren use *The Street* to explore free-time activities and places through pictures, repeated language, and a short classroom task.\n\n### Suggested Activities / Games\n\n1. Family and Feeling Photo Wall: children place family or feeling cards on a wall display; each child chooses one card, acts it out or gives it to a pretend family member, and repeats the useful book phrase.\n2. Kindness Delivery: children deliver a card, hug token, smile token, or bedtime item to a puppet/family picture, naming the item or feeling before placing it.\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n- Look at the picture.\n- Point to the _____.\n- Say it with me.\n- Read with me.\n- What do you see?\n- Good reading.\n\n\n# Lesson 4: Level AA - The Trip\n\n## Core Book: Level AA - The Trip\n\n### Source\n\n- **Book:** Level AA - *The Trip*\n- **PDF:** TBD\n- **Audio:** TBD\n- **Video:** TBD\n- **Lesson Plan:** TBD\n- **Worksheet:** TBD\n\n### Language Pattern\n\n#### Sentence Frame\n\n`The ____.`\n\n#### Key Vocabulary\n\nclothes, suitcase, map, car, seat belt, dog (recurs from U5 L4 Summer Camp), book (recurs from U2 L2 The Classroom), grandparents (recurs as a U1 family member from My Family)\n\n### Story Context\n\nChildren use *The Trip* to explore free-time activities and places through pictures, repeated language, and a short classroom task.\n\n### Suggested Activities / Games\n\n1. School-Day Mission: set up a small classroom or school map with clothes, suitcase, map, car, and seat belt cards; children choose one card, use or echo `The ____.`, and move it to the place where it belongs.\n2. Ready for School Role-Play: children pack, carry, deliver, or arrange the book items in a pretend school routine, then finish by naming 2-3 items from the book together.\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n- Look at the picture.\n- Point to the _____.\n- Say it with me.\n- Read with me.\n- What do you see?\n- Good reading.\n",
